From: Ирганов А.Н.
Date: 2026-10-04
Subject: [PATCH] seL4: map the GOP framebuffer chunk as Write-Combining (PAT)

The framebuffer text console (pc99 io.c) writes glyphs and scrolls the whole
framebuffer on every line; on real hardware (ThinkPad T580) that memory is
effectively uncached, so the boot log rendering dominates boot time. Map the
framebuffer's 1 GiB kernel-window chunk with PAT[4]=WC (PDE.PAT=1) so writes
combine; the IOAPIC/DRHU MMIO in the same chunk stays UC because
map_kernel_window_devices() overrides those pages afterwards.

diff --git a/src/arch/x86/64/kernel/vspace.c b/src/arch/x86/64/kernel/vspace.c
index 5743926..0a70355 100644
--- a/src/arch/x86/64/kernel/vspace.c
+++ b/src/arch/x86/64/kernel/vspace.c
@@ -89,6 +89,13 @@ BOOT_CODE bool_t map_kernel_window(
     /* also map the physical memory into the big kernel window */
     paddr = 0;
     vaddr = PPTR_BASE;
+    /* Schild TCB: map the GOP framebuffer's 1 GiB chunk as Write-Combining
+     * (PAT[4]) so the framebuffer text console is fast. The framebuffer
+     * (Intel stolen memory / UEFI GOP) is effectively uncached otherwise,
+     * which makes the per-character glyph + scroll writes dominate boot time.
+     * The IOAPIC/DRHU MMIO inside the same chunk stays UC because
+     * map_kernel_window_devices() overrides those specific pages afterwards. */
+    paddr_t fb_chunk = boot_state.fb_info.addr & ~(BIT(seL4_HugePageBits) - 1);
     for (paddr = 0; paddr < PADDR_TOP;
          paddr += BIT(seL4_HugePageBits)) {
 
@@ -96,7 +103,7 @@ BOOT_CODE bool_t map_kernel_window(
         x64KSKernelPDPT[pdpte_index] = pdpte_pdpte_1g_new(
                                            0,          /* xd               */
                                            paddr,      /* physical address */
-                                           0,          /* PAT              */
+                                           (paddr == fb_chunk) ? 1 : 0, /* PAT (WC for fb) */
                                            KERNEL_IS_GLOBAL(), /* global   */
                                            0,          /* dirty            */
                                            0,          /* accessed         */
